# Docker
.env

# VSCode specific
*.code-workspace
/.vscode

# Service data
./data
./output


# Python
./.venv

# Compiled bytecode and setuptools output. These were TRACKED -- 36 files, 19
# under comfyfetch/__pycache__, 7 under tests/__pycache__, 10 under
# services/fetch/build/lib -- which is why a routine `git add -A` swept bytecode
# into two pull requests and had to be unpicked by hand both times. The
# `./.venv` line above is anchored to the repo root and never matched them.
#
# Note the leading-slash style used elsewhere in this file is deliberate for
# root-only paths; these are unanchored on purpose, because bytecode appears
# beside whatever it compiles.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
build/

# Scratch the release job writes into the workspace. Ignored so a stray
# `git add -A` in CI can never commit a build artifact back into the repo.
/dist/
/notes.md
/tags.txt
/IMAGE-DIGESTS.txt

# setuptools build detritus. It was tracked, so every `uv build` produced a
# spurious diff and could commit a stale version string back into the repo.
*.egg-info/
